WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:10.060
During spring 2018, tens of thousands of teachers walked out of their schools.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:13.180
These walkouts attracted extensive media attention,

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.440
prompting RJ Martin and I to examine
coverage in five major newspapers.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.080
What did we find? A mix of good and bad.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.660
The good: National papers published
a total of 59 articles on the strikes.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.560
Of these, 56 had impartial
headlines and leads. Impressive!

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.440
The bad: The 254 quotes in the
59 stories favored the strikes

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.180
by a 5 to 1 margin — a ratio that
far exceeded actual public opinion.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.740
Meanwhile, affected families were almost never quoted.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:46.180
Worse: The coverage did little to
put compensation in context

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:50.820
or to explain how teacher salaries stack up against median household income in their state.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.960
Stories also rarely noted the cost of
generous health care and pension plans.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.300
And that's a problem, because these benefits

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.260
are a big part of any serious
conversation about teacher pay.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:04.900
Newspapers can do better, and
with more strikes looming,

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.660
they'll have plenty of chances.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.300
How do you think media covered the teacher strikes?

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:11.880
Let us know in your comments.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.960
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.560
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

